 Reasonable mitigation expenses (whether successful or unsuccessful), such as retaining the services of a placement agency, are recoverable. m. Restitutionary relief. Resti- tutionary relief “off the contract” may be available in certain circumstances. Under § 31 of the Restatement Third, Restitution and Unjust Enrichment, “[a] person who renders performance under an agreement that cannot be en- forced against the recipient by reason of (a) indefiniteness, or (b) the failure to satisfy an extrinsic requirement of enforceability such as the Statute of Frauds, has a claim in restitution against the recipient as necessary to prevent unjust enrichment.” For in- stance, if an employee is discharged in violation of an oral agreement that is not enforceable for lack of a writing under the applicable statute of frauds, the employee may recover in quantum meruit for the reasonable value of ser- vices rendered. § 9.02 Damages—Employer Termination in Breach of a Binding Policy Statement Limiting Termination of Employment An employer who breaches a binding employer policy statement that lim- its termination of employment (§ 2.05) is subject to liability to the dis- charged employee for: (a) all compensation the employee would have received under that policy statement, less mitigation of losses (such as the compensation the employee earned and could have obtained by reasonable efforts in § 9.01 RESTATEMENT OF EMPLOYMENT LAW"